==============================================================================
The dark colour group: uniqueness closed for every N_c
==============================================================================

(1) THE IDENTITY  2(N_c^2-1)/N_c = 2*N_c - 2/N_c
    verified exactly (Fraction arithmetic) for N_c = 1..2000: identical throughout

(2) THE RECORDED TABLE, reproduced
    N_c :        2        3        4        5        6
    N_f :        3    5.333      7.5      9.6    11.67

(3) THE ARGUMENT: 2*N_c is always an integer, so N_f is an integer
    exactly when N_c divides 2.
    scan N_c = 1..100000 for integer N_f -> [1, 2]
    (the scan CONFIRMS the argument; the argument is what proves it,
     since a scan of any finite range never could)
      N_c = 1 -> N_f = 0   [no gluons (N_c^2-1 = 0), no quarks: the empty theory]
      N_c = 2 -> N_f = 3   [the standing assignment]

(4) WHY THE NEAR-MISSES ARE NEAR
    N_f = 2*N_c - 2/N_c sits just under the even integer 2*N_c, by
    2/N_c, which shrinks as N_c grows:
      N_c =   3: 2*N_c =   6, short by 0.6667
      N_c =   6: 2*N_c =  12, short by 0.3333
      N_c =  12: 2*N_c =  24, short by 0.1667
      N_c = 100: 2*N_c = 200, short by 0.0200
    so large-N_c values approach an integer without attaining one —
    the deficit is never zero for N_c > 2.

VERDICT:
   THE UNIQUENESS IS PROVABLE, NOT MERELY TABULATED. N_f is an
   integer iff N_c | 2, so the only candidates in the entire
   infinite range are N_c = 1 (the empty theory, N_f = 0) and
   N_c = 2 (N_f = 3). SU(2) with three light flavours is the unique
   nontrivial solution of the self-cancellation condition, and the
   five-row table can be replaced by one line of divisibility.
   This STRENGTHENS the recorded claim rather than altering it: the
   conclusion was right, the warrant behind it was a finite scan.
==============================================================================
